window.COACH = {"page": "hardware/photometer", "title": "Hardware · Photometer", "prizeName": "Best Hardware", "summary": {"verdict": "On calibration this page is past every Best Hardware winner of 2023 to 2025 and past both iGEM photometer precedents: two standards, slope, intercept, R squared and mean difference tabulated by range, an audit of the reference instrument until its ceiling is found, and then a refusal to take the flattering reading. What holds it back on paper is bookkeeping, and most of it is a few hours of work with no new experiments: eighteen cost cells that say not costed yet while the Summary quotes $150, no assembly order, no firmware link, no CAD source, no licence, and nobody outside the hardware team who has touched the instrument. The harder problem is the 400 h run the page opens on, because our own Digital Twin page gives it a different length, a different point count, a different strain and a different cuvette, and marks its OD channel unusable from 243.0 h, which is 93 hours inside the window you traced.", "strengths": ["The calibration audits its own reference instrument. \"from 70 mg onward it returns exactly 2.50 at every single point, which is a ceiling, not a measurement\", then the 14% direct-against-dilution disagreement, then the seven steps in twenty where a weighed standard reads lower as more of it is added. No precedent in either evidence bank questions its reference at all.", "A wrong diagnosis kept on the page. \"We first guessed contamination, a second organism taking over, and that was wrong: the recirculation pump had failed.\" None of the five Best Hardware winners admits a misdiagnosis anywhere.", "Limits that name the test which was skipped, not only the result that is missing. \"the flush and reblank that would have told fouling from biology was never run, so the shape of the curve is reported as observed\", and in 8.1 three verbs in order: \"has not been purchased, integrated or tested\".", "The wavelength derived from a constraint in the biology. You give both CcaS peaks, 535 nm and 672 nm, the position chosen between them, and the consequence of getting it wrong, which is a circuit switching itself on every ten minutes for weeks.", "Eight STLs numbered consistently across the exploded view, the part list and the download block, with a print-settings table giving printer, nozzle, plate, profile, layer height, infill, supports and line width. Neither photometer precedent publishes a single print setting."], "priorities": ["Settle the long run with the Digital Twin team before anything is frozen. Five facts about one run disagree across our own wiki: more than 400 h against 434 h, 2,132 points against 2,671 valid rows, WB800 against B. subtilis 168, a 0.2 mm cuvette against a 2 mm one, and a failed recirculation pump against a drop the twin's trust layer scored as a process event \"with the optical channel normal\". The twin and Results pages also mark the OD channel unusable from 243.0 h, inside your traced 336 h window. Whichever way each one falls, both pages have to say the same thing.", "Cost the bill of materials, or delete the cost columns. Eighteen rows read \"not costed yet\" including the total, while the Summary says \"estimated at $150\" and 7.1 says $150 against $15,000 with no source or date for either. Aachen 2014 is the format to copy: quantity, unit cost, line total, a subtotal per build, and the exchange rate with the date it was taken. An empty column does more damage than one honest sentence.", "Check the silica run's volume, then tell the rest of the wiki the run happened. You write 500 mL of 1% SDS; the written protocol our Results page describes \"holds volume constant at 50 mL\", and only 50 mL fits the extinction estimate that same page cites, where 1 mg/mL is about OD600 2.3. Every concentration in 5.2, 5.3 and 5.4 rests on it. Meanwhile Results still says \"The run has not happened\", Measurement calls it \"a written protocol with no results behind it yet\", and Engineering calls it \"Cycle 7 ... deferred\".", "Write one assembly sequence and link the code. Figure 10's caption already names the two gaps that stop a rebuild, the 330 ohm resistor and the two sensor addresses, and 8.2 holds the one assembly warning in the wrong section. Add the three optical spacings from photometer cycle 1, a licence line for the designs, and the firmware link, and the fourth ballot question is answered.", "Put one named person outside the hardware team in front of the instrument. Section 7.1 offers it to \"any student or professor\" and nobody outside the sub-team is recorded as having assembled or read it. Aachen 2014 had team Freiburg repeat part of its calibration, which is the only answer to this ballot question anywhere in the evidence bank. One afternoon of somebody building from 9.1 and writing down where they got stuck answers the user-testing question and tests the documentation question at the same time."], "ballot": [{"question": "Does the hardware you developed address a need or problem in synthetic biology?", "status": "strong", "where": "Summary, and 02 Optical Design 2.1", "note": "The instrument exists to read OD600 without opening the loop, and 2.1 derives its wavelength from the optogenetic circuit it must not trigger, which is a need that comes from inside the project instead of a general one."}, {"question": "Did the team conduct user testing and learn from user feedback?", "status": "missing", "where": "", "note": "No person outside the hardware team is named anywhere on the page as having assembled, run or read the instrument, and 7.1 offers it to any student or professor."}, {"question": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?", "status": "strong", "where": "05 Calibration, and 01 Final Product 1.3", "note": "Forty silica points across three instruments with slope, intercept, R squared, resolution and a stated working range, plus a long in-line run, is more functional evidence than any winner in the bank. It is held back by the run's numbers disagreeing with the Digital Twin page and by the three requirements in 3.1 that have no reported test."}, {"question": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?", "status": "partial", "where": "01 Final Product 1.1, and 09 Design Files", "note": "Part numbers for the electronics, eight STLs and a complete print-settings table, against an uncosted parts table, no supplier or specification for any of the five optical parts, no assembly order, no firmware, no CAD source and no licence on the designs."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["Summary", "Results", "01 Final Product", "02 Optical Design", "03 Mechanical Design", "04 Electronics and Firmware", "05 Calibration", "06 Simplified Photometer", "07 Specifications and Future Implications", "08 Limitations", "09 Design Files"], "winners": ["Need: what the work requires, what is used now, what that costs or cannot do", "Overview of the device, often as a short list of its key properties", "Design and build, ordered by prototype or by subsystem", "Demonstration: one run that shows the device does its job", "Reproduction: parts, costs, files, instructions"], "gap": "Four of the five slots are here, and your demonstration slot is stronger than any winner's, including Cornell, which has none. Two things sit in the wrong order. The need slot is thin and late: the Summary opens on the mechanism and the $15,000 comparison arrives in the third sentence, where four of the five winners open on the barrier. And the reproduction slot is split between Part 1 and Part 9, with the costs missing from one end and the assembly missing from both. Part 6 then proposes a simplified build that Part 5 has already measured, so the page argues forwards and then back."}, "extras": ["The scroll panel says \"2,132 points across the run, no samples withdrawn\" where the Summary says those points cover \"the first 336\". Write \"2,132 points over the first 336 hours\".", "Section 5.2 refers to \"the slim build described in the previous section\" and 5.3 does the same. The slim build is described in Part 6, which comes after Part 5. Point both at Part 6, or move Part 6 in front of the calibration.", "Figure 14's caption says \"open points are where its value is reconstructed from a four-fold dilution\", and the text says reconstruction starts with a two-fold dilution at 22.5 mg. Name both dilutions in the caption.", "Section 5.1 gives no volume for the two TiO2 runs. Our Results page says \"Cumulative additions from 2 to 60 mg in 400 mL\" and our bioreactor and Engineering pages both describe a 500 mL reservoir. Pick one and put it in 5.1.", "Section 5.2 never names the standard it was run against. Our Measurement page gives \"Tomicro; mean particle diameter 1 um; stock concentration 25 mg/mL\". Since 5.4's caveat turns on how silica scatters compared with cells, the particle size belongs in 5.2's first sentence.", "The Measurement page's written silica protocol uses deionized water and this page uses 1% SDS. Say which was run, because SDS changes how the particles disperse.", "The specification sheet has no working range, and 5.2 settled it. Add one row: \"Working range: absolute OD600 to 2.5; above that, an index of density (5.2)\".", "\"Body cover\" and \"Body cover, small\" in 9.1 carry the identical description, \"Cover that blocks external light along the light path.\" Say which stretch of the path each one covers.", "The parts table reads \"3x 12 in + 1 in 20 AWG black\" for four wire colours. It is not clear whether that is a 12 in piece and a 1 in piece or 13 in of wire. Give the two lengths on separate lines.", "The 2510 connectors and the DuPont crimp pins are listed without saying what mates with what. One Notes column saying which connector joins the PCB to the sensors and which to the Arduino would make the harness buildable.", "\"Paralleling lens\" is your term throughout and the usual word is collimating lens. Keep yours and add the standard one once in 2.1 or 3.5, so a judge outside optics can follow the part.", "\"Matching that wavelength makes our readings comparable with the BioDrop, with published growth curves, and with anything a judge or another lab would expect from our photometer.\" Cut the last clause: the first two are the argument, and addressing the judge inside a technical reason weakens it.", "Section 2.2's \"The stronger beam passes through the flow cuvette, which holds the continually flowing medium, before it reaches its lux sensor\" repeats the sentence in front of it. Delete it.", "\"That makes the median a bubble-proof estimate of the true value\" claims more than Figure 11's own caption allows: \"a clean burst can still lose a reading, because on a burst of fourteen the MAD is itself a noisy estimate.\" Write \"a bubble-resistant estimate\".", "No licence appears on the designs. The footer's CC BY 4.0 covers the page text, and the repository link in it is the wiki's source. Add one line under 9.1 naming the licence the STLs are released under.", "The page never mentions another team's instrument. TU Darmstadt 2019's SensorBrick is an in-line OD600 sensor on a reactor loop, the same class as ours, and it stops the flow to read where we read it moving. Aachen 2014's OD/F device is the DIY photometer most later iGEM builds credit. One sentence naming both and saying what you did differently would size the contribution."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["model", "software", "protein-design", "protein-design/methodology", "protein-design/sprints", "protein-design/molecular-dynamics", "hardware/bioreactor", "hardware/photometer", "hardware/diopal", "hardware/hydroponics"], "wiki": "655c7af, 2026-10-12", "root": "../../", "notes": [{"id": "ph-001", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Every reading is taken through the glass, on culture that is still moving.", "title": "The one thing that makes it different", "body": "This is the whole instrument in one line: the culture stays in the loop, and the reading is taken on it while it moves. A judge who reads nothing else knows what you built and what it avoids. Keep this sentence wherever the page is cut.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-01"], "excerpt_note": "TU Darmstadt's in-line OD sensor stops the flow to read it. You read it moving, and this line is where you can say so.", "ballot": "Does the hardware you developed address a need or problem in synthetic biology?"}, {"id": "ph-002", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The photometer reads OD600 from the flowing culture without ever drawing a sample.", "title": "Open on who needs this, then the device", "body": "This opens on the mechanism. Four of the five Best Hardware winners open on the need: what the work requires, what is used now, and what that costs. You already have the material one line down, in the $15,000 BioDrop. Put the barrier first and the device second.", "after": "Measuring OD600 means stopping a culture and taking a sample out of it, and the benchtop instrument that reads it costs $15,000. Our photometer reads OD600 from the flowing culture without ever drawing a sample.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-01", "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-01"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen opens on price and on who is shut out by it, before the device is named. TU Darmstadt builds the need in four moves and ends on what the prior art failed to do.", "ballot": "Does the hardware you developed address a need or problem in synthetic biology?"}, {"id": "ph-003", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "shines through a 0.2 mm flow cuvette plumbed into the bioreactor", "title": "Our two pages disagree tenfold", "body": "You say 0.2 mm here and on the bioreactor page. Our Digital Twin page says 2 mm three times, and its own arithmetic depends on it: \"the dual-beam splitter at 9:1 with a path-length factor of 5.0, so a 2 mm cell is reported on a 1 cm scale\". Ten over two is five, so that factor only works for a 2 mm cell. Path length scales OD directly, so one of the two numbers makes every OD600 on both pages wrong by ten. Measure the cuvette and write the same number in all three places.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-004", "type": "praise", "anchor": "reported OD600 is the ratio of the two sensors, so drift in the LED divides out", "title": "The second sensor's reason, in one clause", "body": "You give the design choice and the thing it buys in the same breath, so a reader never has to ask why there are two sensors. No winner page explains a reference channel this economically.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-005", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "It ran in line for more than 400 hours, 2,132 points logged over the first 336", "title": "The same run, two sets of numbers", "body": "Our Digital Twin page describes what looks like this run as \"21 July to 8 August 2026: 434 hours of B. subtilis 168 at 22 degrees C, 2,671 valid rows, no sample drawn\". You say more than 400 hours and 2,132 points over the first 336. If these are the same run, name the full length once and say which window you traced. If they are different runs, say so here, because a judge reading both pages will assume they are one.", "after": "It ran in line for 434 hours, with nothing withdrawn from the culture. The 2,132 points traced below are the first 336 hours of that run.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-006", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The parts are estimated at $150 against the $15,000 BioDrop it was blanked against.", "title": "The estimate and the empty table fight", "body": "The next sentence says the bill of materials is not costed, so the page gives a price and then withdraws it. Neither figure has a source or a date. Aachen 2014's photometer table carries quantity, unit cost in two currencies, a line total, a subtotal per build, and the exchange rate with the date it was taken, which is where its \"$60\" comes from. An uncosted table is worse than no table, because the structure promises a number that never arrives.", "after": "The parts come to about $150 [total from Table 1], against the $15,000 BioDrop it was blanked against [source, date].", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-04", "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-04"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt gives the reason for publishing a price and then one figure a team can hold against its own budget. Aachen ties the cost claim to the table above it and to the one variable the reader controls.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-007", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The long in-line run Measured Past 400 h at 22 °C", "title": "Sort this strip by what it is", "body": "This strip mixes finished measurements with confidence labels, so Measured, Observed, Partial and Untested all read at the same weight. Aachen 2015's front door is seven bullets, each a capability with its measured range. Put the three measured results together with their numbers, then the two partials, then the untested row.", "after": "The long in-line run Measured 434 h at 22 °C; 2,132 points traced over the first 336 h, no sample withdrawn\nBubble and outlier filter Measured 14-reading bursts cut at median ± 3·MAD; 28.6% rejected on day 10\nThe 10° tilt Measured Almost all bubbles left the cuvette within about a quarter of a second\n(then the two Partial rows, then the Untested row)", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt's opening doubles as a specification sheet: configurations, volumes, wavelength range. A reader decides in one paragraph whether the instrument fits.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-008", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "no comparison is tabulated on this page", "title": "Section five tabulates three comparisons", "body": "This row says no comparison is tabulated. Section 5.2 has Table 2 (slope, intercept, R squared and mean difference over three ranges), Table 3 (each instrument against weighed mass) and Table 4 (resolution). The row was written before the silica run and has not caught up. It is the strongest evidence on the page, so this row should point at it.", "after": "Agreement with the BioDrop Measured One to one to OD 2.5: slope 0.995, intercept -0.07, mean difference -0.08 OD (Table 2)", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-009", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Its OD readings are reported to deviate little or not at all", "title": "Your own data now says how much", "body": "This is a hedge, and section 5.3 replaced it with numbers: R squared 0.992 against silica mass, a 5.57 times scale factor, a mean difference of -0.10 OD below OD 2.5, and no reference beam. \"Reported to deviate little or not at all\" now reads as if nobody had measured it.", "after": "The compact redesign Measured On one silica series it is linear across the whole range, R squared 0.992, but reads on a 5.57 times compressed scale with no reference beam; not integrated into the bioreactor for lack of time", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-010", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Range sensors bought; no multi-wavelength source purchased, integrated or tested", "title": "An untested row that names what is missing", "body": "Three verbs, in order, and the one thing that exists is separated from the three that do not. A reader knows precisely how far the work got. This is the hardest habit on a hardware page and you have it in the summary strip, where it is most visible.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06"], "excerpt_note": "The 2025 overgraduate winner reports its own OD sensor as built but neither tested nor integrated, in the paragraph that describes it."}, {"id": "ph-011", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "28.6% rejected on day 10, under the 50% alarm", "title": "Say whether that rate is normal", "body": "28.6% of a 14-reading burst is four readings thrown out of every fourteen. A reader cannot tell whether that is the bubbles the filter exists for or a cut that is too tight, and the demonstration in Figure 11 rejects one of fourteen with a bubble in it. Give the rejection rate at the start of the run as well, so the reader sees whether it rose.", "after": "Bubble and outlier filter Measured 14-reading bursts cut at median ± 3·MAD; [number]% rejected at the start of the run and 28.6% by day 10, both under the 50% alarm", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-012", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The animation at the top of the page follows the beam through the optical parts", "title": "Say what the product is before listing it", "body": "Part 1 is called Final Product and opens with an 18-row parts table. The winners put the overview first: what the device is, in one paragraph, then the parts. Your 1.2 is that paragraph. Swap 1.1 and 1.2 so the table arrives after the reader knows what it builds.", "after": "Open Part 1 with 1.2's first paragraph, then the exploded view, then the bill of materials. The animation at the top of the page follows the beam through the optical parts; the pictures in the table are the parts' own CAD models, in the animation's colours.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-04"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen introduces its parts table by splitting the build into custom and off-the-shelf, so the reader knows what the table is for before reading a row of it."}, {"id": "ph-013", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "itemised to the individual component with part numbers and unit costs in US dollars", "title": "There are no unit costs in this table", "body": "Every cost cell in the table reads \"not costed yet\", the total row included. This sentence promises unit costs in US dollars, so a judge arrives at the table expecting them. Either cost the table or change this sentence. The research found this is the single largest gap on the page, and it is a few hours with a receipt file, not a new experiment.", "after": "The full bill of materials is below, itemised to the individual component with part numbers. Unit costs are not yet recorded.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-014", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Light path in the order the light meets it, as in Figure 6", "title": "The optical parts cannot be bought from this", "body": "You give part numbers for the GY-302 sensors, the Arduino Uno Q and the UTC PN2222, which is exactly right. The five optical rows give none: the LED is \"1x 600 nm\" with no supplier or forward voltage, and the lens, splitter and cuvette have no focal length, no dimensions and no window material. Those are the parts that set the measurement. Evry won an overgraduate award with no costs at all, because it named every part precisely enough to buy.", "after": "| LED | 1x 600 nm, [supplier, part number], [forward voltage], [viewing angle]", "excerpts": ["hardware-2023-OG-evry-01"], "excerpt_note": "Evry names replication as a design constraint that drove component choice, and then carries part numbers for nearly everything.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-015", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "so they are reminded when medium or a refill of our bacteria must be added", "title": "The farmer needs a number here", "body": "This promises the instrument tells a farmer when to feed the reactor, and nothing on the page says at what OD600 that happens or how the reminder reaches them. Section 4.2's only alarm is for bubbles. Either give the threshold or say the reminder is future work.", "after": "The photometer sits inside our larger bioreactor and reports the bacterial density in it continuously. Turning that reading into a feeding reminder at a set OD600 is still to be built.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the hardware you developed address a need or problem in synthetic biology?"}, {"id": "ph-016", "type": "praise", "anchor": "the numbers match the design files in 9.1", "title": "One numbering scheme from drawing to download", "body": "The exploded view, the eight part names above it and the download list in 9.1 all use the same numbers, and the caption says which image is CAD and which is a photograph of the build. A reader can go from a part in the drawing to the file that prints it without guessing. Two of the five winners claim CAD files and link none.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-017", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Its first 336 hours, 2,132 logged points, are traced in Figure 2 from the live dashboard.", "title": "Our wiki calls part of this window unusable", "body": "Our Digital Twin page scores this run and says \"The trust layer flagged the sensor at 216.3 h, 26.7 h before the OD channel failed completely at 243.0 h\", and its window table reads \"240-434 h | Reference collapses; OD scatter 5 to 10x higher | Photometer and OD both unreliable\". Our Results page marks \"the point at 243.0 h after which the OD channel cannot be used\". Your traced window runs to 336 h, so 93 hours of this figure is data two other ReLEAF pages call unusable. Settle which window is sound and trace that one. While you are there, link the log file: the Digital Twin page cites out/sentinel.json and this page traces a screenshot.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-018", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "engineered Bacillus subtilis WB800 at 22 °C, read in line through the flow cell", "title": "Two pages, two strains, one run", "body": "You say engineered B. subtilis WB800. Our Digital Twin page says \"434 hours of B. subtilis 168 at 22 degrees C\" and states a rule that settles it either way: \"Every reactor run before October used B. subtilis 168.\" The run started on 21 July. The temperature, the no-sampling claim and the loop all match, so this is one run with two strains written on it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-019", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "a 195.54 lx sample against a 26.67 lx reference, a ratio of 7.33×", "title": "You measured the drift you built this for", "body": "The reference channel is 26.67 lx here at hour 30 and 25.83 lx in Figure 12 at hour 244. That is a 3% fall in the lamp over 214 hours, measured, on the run the page is about. Section 2.2 justifies the whole reference beam on an LED that \"degrades over years\" and never reports the drift it caught. One sentence with those two numbers is the strongest evidence on the page that the splitter earns its cost.", "after": "The reference channel read 26.67 lx at hour 30 and 25.83 lx at hour 244, a fall of about 3% in the source over 214 hours. That fall divides out of the ratio, which is what the reference beam is for.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-03"], "excerpt_note": "TU Darmstadt separates calibration from verification in one sentence, then reports the verification. Same move: the design claim and the measurement that tests it, side by side.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-020", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "the recirculation pump had failed, which a teammate confirmed on inspection", "title": "Our Digital Twin page gives a different cause", "body": "You attribute the drop to a failed pump. Our Digital Twin page scores a drop on this run as biology: \"The 120-160 h drop is real: 0.21 OD against a local residual SD of 0.022, with the optical channel normal. Contamination found no support\", and it leaves autolysis, sporulation and biofilm sloughing open. It then records a separate instrument event from 216.3 h. If those are two different drops, give the hour of yours. If they are one, the two pages disagree about whether it was the pump or the culture.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-021", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Optical density alone cannot separate lysis from sporulation or settling", "title": "The measurement's limit and the skipped test", "body": "You name what OD600 cannot tell apart, then name the specific test that would have told fouling from biology and say it was never run, then label the curve as observed. Three moves in one sentence, and the claim ends up exactly as strong as the evidence. TU Darmstadt's limitation paragraph is the best in the whole precedent bank and it does the same thing.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-05"], "excerpt_note": "Darmstadt gives the threshold, downgrades its own claim to qualitative, then says why the downgraded claim still does the job."}, {"id": "ph-022", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The run passed 400 hours.", "title": "Say whether the membrane module was fitted", "body": "Our bioreactor page says \"the longest run with the membrane module fitted is about 53 hours\" and that \"The 400 h photometer record was drawn from the bioreactor loop\". A judge who reads both will take this for a 400-hour bioreactor run unless you say otherwise. One clause protects both pages.", "after": "The run passed 400 hours on the lumen loop, with the membrane module not fitted.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-023", "type": "praise", "anchor": "right in the middle of the green (~535 nm) and red (~672 nm) peaks of CcaS", "title": "A wavelength chosen against a stated constraint", "body": "You give the two peaks that must not be hit, the position you chose between them, and the consequence of getting it wrong, which is the circuit switching itself on every ten minutes for weeks. This is a requirement sitting next to a capability, and it is the move the 2025 undergraduate winner was rewarded for.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell gives what the part delivers, what the organism needs, and what was actually used, in three numbers.", "ballot": "Does the hardware you developed address a need or problem in synthetic biology?"}, {"id": "ph-024", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Every report would have had limitations had we worked at 700 nm.", "title": "This sentence adds nothing", "body": "The three sentences before it already say why 600 nm is the comparable choice. This one introduces a wavelength nobody proposed and a limitation it does not name. Delete it.", "after": "Matching that wavelength makes our readings comparable with the BioDrop and with published growth curves.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-025", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Our OD600 readings are not really measuring absorbed light; they are purely turbidity.", "title": "The same point three times in one bullet", "body": "This bullet says scattering not absorbance three times: here, then \"by how much light scatters off the cells, not by what light they absorb\", then the clearance from protein and nucleic-acid absorbance. Your editor's rule is to keep one. The first clause of the bullet and the last sentence carry it.", "after": "It measures turbidity. Little absorbs 600 nm light, but cells scatter it strongly, so we read how many bacteria are in the bioreactor from how much light scatters off them. At 600 nm we are clear of protein and nucleic-acid absorbance in the UV, and of most of the medium's absorbance, which is in the blue.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-026", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "A light that comes on every 10 minutes, over runs of days, weeks or even months", "title": "The sampling interval is hiding here", "body": "This is the only place the page says how often it reads, and it appears inside an argument about optogenetics. The specification sheet cites 2.1 for \"About every 10 minutes\", so a reader following that reference lands in a paragraph about CcaS. Put the duty cycle in 4.2 with the burst, and keep the cross-talk argument here.", "after": "A light that switches on for every burst, over runs of days, weeks or even months, would be a major problem if it actuated the circuit.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-027", "type": "cut", "anchor": "These calculations are built into our software", "title": "Two claims with nothing behind them", "body": "The ratio arithmetic is already in 4.3, and \"keeps the interface easy to navigate for a small-scale farmer\" is a usability claim on a page with no usability test. An optics section is not where either belongs.", "after": "The splitter gives us a reference sensor that measures 10% of the light directly from the source, to compare against the sample sensor, which then only has to measure the scattering of the bacteria in solution. Section 4.3 gives the arithmetic.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-028", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "an LED degrades over years", "title": "Your own page hedges this twice", "body": "Here the degradation is stated as fact over years. Section 4.3 says the ratio corrects for drift \"if the LED degrades\", and the longest run is 17 days. The honest and stronger version is the measurement in Figure 4 and Figure 12: the reference fell from 26.67 to 25.83 lx in 214 hours.", "after": "An LED's output falls as it runs, and ours fell about 3% over the 400 h run (Figure 4, Figure 12). The splitter gives us a reference sensor that measures 10% of the light directly from the source.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-029", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Neither the sample nor the reference lux sensor could be affected by changes in external lighting", "title": "This requirement has no reported result", "body": "This is a pass-or-fail requirement and the page never reports the test. Section 3.2 says you tested by switching off the lights and closing the doors, which removes the thing the requirement is about. With the covers on, two readings, lights on and lights off, settle it in two minutes. UBC's leak and bubble tests each carry a stated pass condition and a result, and that is what the third ballot question rewards.", "after": "Neither sensor could be affected by changes in external lighting. With the covers fitted, the sample sensor reads [number] lx in a lit room and [number] lx in the dark, a difference of [number]%.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-UG-ubc-01"], "excerpt_note": "UBC fixes the condition that makes a reading repeatable before quoting any number.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-030", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "had to stay free of bubbles for most of the testing period", "title": "Most of the time is not a requirement", "body": "A requirement needs a line someone can check. Two numbers elsewhere on the page are candidates: the quarter of a second in 3.4 and the 50% rejection alarm in 4.2. Use one of them here.", "after": "The flow cuvette had to clear a bubble fast enough that the burst filter still had clean readings to work with, which we set at under 50% of readings rejected.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-031", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "While modelling the photometer in 3D we planned a clear path of improvement.", "title": "Lead with the choice, not the plan", "body": "The paragraph's point is in its second half: you started loose so parts could be moved by hand, then locked them once the positions were known. The opening sentence announces a plan instead of making it.", "after": "We knew the photometer needed darkness, an enclosed path and a stiff structure. Early on we needed to move the sensors and lenses by hand more than we needed any of that, so we built loose first and locked the positions later.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-032", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Tightening them in large steps risked wasting whole prints", "title": "The constraint that ruled out the obvious fix", "body": "You name the problem (loose seats), then the reason the obvious fix was too expensive (whole prints wasted on small steps), then the material property that solved it. A reader can reuse this reasoning on a part you never printed, which is what makes it worth reading.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-UG-ubc-02"], "excerpt_note": "UBC turns a build annoyance into a transferable rule about tolerance on parts that have to fit."}, {"id": "ph-033", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "the change gave us a large jump in data accuracy across long test runs", "title": "Say what you measured, and in what", "body": "A tighter seat holds the optics still, which changes repeatability, and section 5.1 says the readings needed no correction at any point, so accuracy against the BioDrop did not move. Give the scatter before and after if you have it, or say the readings stopped shifting when the instrument was knocked.", "after": "After the change the readings stopped shifting when the housing was moved, and point-to-point scatter over a long run fell from [number] to [number] lx.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-034", "type": "praise", "anchor": "That change did not make the drastic difference we had hoped for.", "title": "A fix that did not work, kept in", "body": "Vertical was your idea and you report that it underdelivered, in the sentence right before the tilt that did work. Keeping the step that failed is what makes the 10 degrees read as a finding instead of a decision. No winner page in the bank admits a middle step this plainly.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-OG-aachen-05"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen reports an attempted improvement that showed no benefit and says why they reverted."}, {"id": "ph-035", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "made almost all of the bubbles leave within about a quarter of a second", "title": "Say how the bubbles were counted", "body": "This number carries the orientation decision and the Results strip, and the page never says how it was observed: how many bubbles, at what flow rate, timed by eye or from video, and what \"almost all\" leaves behind. Give the count and the method in one clause and the claim becomes checkable.", "after": "At 10 degrees, [number] of [number] bubbles cleared the cuvette within about a quarter of a second at the loop's normal flow rate, timed from [video at N frames per second].", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-04", "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-01"], "excerpt_note": "Darmstadt makes a qualitative effect into a test by removing the observers' knowledge of which sample was which. UBC fixes the condition first.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-036", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "has not yet proved unreliable", "title": "Two negatives where one fact will do", "body": "\"Has not yet proved unreliable\" asks the reader to work out what you mean. Say what the tilt has survived.", "after": "The tilt has stayed with the photometer through every later iteration, including the 400 h run.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-037", "type": "cut", "anchor": "the beam splitter, which splits the one light path in two", "title": "The splitter is explained for the fourth time", "body": "The scroll panel at the top, the Summary and the whole of 2.2 have each described the 90:10 split and the reference beam. This list only needs the part named and its place in the order. Your editor's rule is to keep one telling.", "after": "the beam splitter (2.2);", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-038", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "the 2 mm light slit, which narrows the light reaching the sensors", "title": "The slit is in no parts list", "body": "The slit appears here and in the specification sheet, and it is not one of the eight printed parts, not in the bill of materials, and not in any figure caption. A reader rebuilding this cannot tell whether it is printed into the sensor frame, cut from card, or bought. Say which part carries it, and what \"more specific and accurate readings\" means in practice.", "after": "the 2 mm light slit, printed into [part name, 9.1], which narrows the beam to the part of it that crosses the cuvette squarely;", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-039", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Most of it, however, is empty space, needed for the light to reach the focus of the lens", "title": "Three numbers would make this buildable", "body": "The one thing a rebuilder needs from this section is the geometry: the lens focal length, the LED-to-lens distance and the lens-to-cuvette distance. Figure 9 says \"Lengths are measured on the CAD\", which means the reader has to open the STL and measure. Our bioreactor page says photometer cycle 1 produced \"Three measured spacings that became the fixed geometry of every later build\", so the numbers exist. Put them here.", "after": "Most of it is empty space, needed for the light to reach the focus of the lens: the LED sits [number] mm from the lens, and the lens [number] mm from the cuvette, with a focal length of [number] mm.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen's build prose carries the dimensions a maker needs, down to a 7 by 7 mm piece of filter foil.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-040", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Our final photometer does not use a breadboard", "title": "The heading and the long run say otherwise", "body": "The heading above this sentence is \"Breadboard, Sensors, LED\", and Figure 3's caption says the final assembly ran the long run \"wired on a breadboard\". Figure 1's photograph is also described as wired to an Arduino on the bench. So either the 400 h run was done on a breadboard and the PCB came later, or the captions are wrong. Rename the heading and say which build produced the data in 1.3.", "after": "Our final photometer does not use a breadboard: it uses a soldered PCB connected to an Arduino Uno Q. The 400 h run in 1.3 was logged on the breadboard build that preceded it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-041", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "so it runs our code from a power supply alone, with no computer plugged in", "title": "Link the code and say who wrote it", "body": "Sections 4.1, 4.2 and 4.3 describe what the firmware does in detail and the page never links it. Our Digital Twin page credits the program on this board to someone else: \"the Arduino App Lab application our instructor Chars wrote for the Uno Q board on BR-01\". Both precedents ship their firmware, Aachen as two Arduino sketches and TU Darmstadt on GitLab. A link and a one-line credit fix both problems at once.", "after": "The Uno Q is a small computer in itself, so it runs the measurement sketch from a power supply alone, with no computer plugged in. The sketch is at [link]; the App Lab application that runs the rig was written by [name].", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen lists its downloads as a block, with a separate Arduino sketchbook per build, and states the one file it could not publish and why.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-042", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Not drawn: the 330 Ω resistor in Table 1", "title": "This caption lists what blocks a rebuild", "body": "You name the two gaps honestly, and both of them stop a reader wiring the instrument. The 330 ohm resistor is the LED's current limit and its position decides whether the LED survives. The two sensor addresses decide whether you read one sensor twice. Both are one clause each, and after them the wiring is complete.", "after": "D3 switches the LED through the PN2222, with the 330 ohm resistor in series with the LED between [pin] and [pin]. Both GY-302 sensors share 3.3 V, SDA and SCL; the sample sensor's ADDR pin is held [high or low] to put it at 0x[address] and the reference sensor's at 0x[address].", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen's assembly step is written for someone holding the parts, including the warning not to leave a hole open.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-043", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "One shared ground. The Arduino has a limited number of ground ports", "title": "Four principles, one wiring table", "body": "These four bolded paragraphs each carry one real connection fact wrapped in a reason. A reader wiring the board has to hold all four in their head at once and then reconstruct the net list. Keep the four headings as the rows of a table with the connection in one column and the reason in another, and the section gets shorter and more useful at the same time.", "after": "| Net | Connection | Why |\n| Ground | Sensor, LED and transistor grounds joined to one Arduino ground pin | The Arduino has few ground ports |\n| LED switch | D3 to PN2222 base through 1.2 kohm; 10 kohm base to emitter | Lets the LED run at its own voltage and switch without disturbing the sensors |", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-044", "type": "praise", "anchor": "because the bubble drags the average upward, but the median (the middle value) rarely moves", "title": "The median explained to a stranger", "body": "You define the median inside the sentence that needs it, then give the reason it beats the mean here, in words with no statistics in them. None of the four precedent instruments documents its outlier handling at all, so this section has no counterpart anywhere in the bank. Keep the parenthesis.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-045", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "we take the 13 distances of the points from the median", "title": "Fourteen readings give fourteen distances", "body": "A burst of 14 readings has 14 absolute deviations from the median, not 13. If you drop one on purpose, say which and why, because a reader reimplementing this will get a different MAD from yours and will not know whose is right.", "after": "With the median of the burst found, we take the 14 distances of the points from the median.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-046", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "A burst of fourteen. We take 14 lux readings in a short burst.", "title": "Our Digital Twin page describes a different burst", "body": "Our Digital Twin page documents the same firmware as \"Four dark readings, LED on for 60 ms, sixteen more; bubbles rejected by median absolute deviation, and the burst retried if over half is rejected\". That is four dark readings plus sixteen lit ones, a 60 ms LED pulse and a retry. You describe fourteen readings, no dark readings, no pulse length, and a red warning instead of a retry. One of the two is the firmware that ran. The dark readings matter most, because subtracting them is what makes the instrument blind to room light, which is a requirement in 3.1.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-047", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Take 14 lux readings in a burst", "title": "The four steps repeat the four paragraphs", "body": "The numbered strip says the same four things as the paragraphs directly above it, in the same order. Your editor's rule is that when the prose and the box beneath it say the same thing, keep one. The strip is the better summary, so shorten the prose or drop the strip.", "after": "", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-048", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "under the 50% alarm and so not in red", "title": "Two numbers in this caption need a sentence", "body": "A reader meets 28.6% rejected and a ratio in red on the one run the page is built on, and the caption explains neither. Say what the expected 10:1 ratio means, why a culture at OD 1.47 should not produce it, and whether the red figure is a fault or the dashboard expecting the wrong thing. As written, your own screenshot looks like a warning you ignored. Our Digital Twin page reads the fall in that reference channel as a sensor alarm, so this is worth getting right.", "after": "Figure 12 The rejection rate, live The long run's dashboard ten days in (10 d 4 h 9 m, 1,574 points), its rejection rate outlined: 28.6%, under the 50% alarm and so not in red. Beside it the dashboard flags the sample-to-reference ratio in red, because it expects about 10:1 from the 90:10 split and reads 4.64×. [State here whether that is the culture attenuating the sample beam as designed, or the source falling, which is what the reference channel is there to catch.]", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-049", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "With K captured, every live reading is converted with:", "title": "This formula does not give your dashboard numbers", "body": "Work the formula back from your own two screenshots. Figure 4 reads 195.54 lx sample, 26.67 lx reference, OD600 0.4834, which needs K of about 22.3. Figure 12 reads 120 lx, 25.83 lx, OD600 1.4746, which needs K of about 138.6. K is a blank taken once, so it cannot be both, and neither is near the 10:1 the dashboard says it expects. Either the formula is missing a term, such as the path-length factor the Digital Twin page uses, or the blank was retaken mid-run. Add one worked example with real numbers so a reader can check their own build.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-050", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "at the start of a live reading, we take a blank and calculate K", "title": "Say when a blank is taken", "body": "\"At the start of a live reading\" could mean once per run or once per burst, and those are different instruments. A reader also needs to know what goes in the cuvette (medium, not water, which is the correction your bioreactor page records) and whether K was retaken during the 400 h run. Section 5.4 says the slim build \"should be blanked more often\", which only means something once this is fixed.", "after": "Before a run starts we take one blank on the nutrient medium with no bacteria in it, and calculate K from it. K is held for the whole run unless the loop is opened.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-051", "type": "cut", "anchor": "The sample sensor reports the light transmitted through the culture", "title": "The two sensors have been introduced twice", "body": "The Summary and the whole of 2.2 have already said which sensor sees the culture and which does not. This section only needs to start at the arithmetic.", "after": "Each sensor returns an illuminance in lux. Reported OD600 comes from the ratio of sample to reference, which normalises every reading against the LED's output.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-052", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A photometer is only worth the reference it has been checked against.", "title": "The principle stated before the method", "body": "This opening and the three sentences after it argue for a primary standard over an instrument comparison, and they give the physical reason: a culture changes while it is being measured, and weighed mineral particles do not. None of the four precedent instruments states this at all, and all four calibrate against another machine. A judge who reads only this paragraph knows your calibration was designed and not improvised.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-053", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "across two runs the raw, uncalibrated output tracked the BioDrop to a median paired difference of 3.8%", "title": "Both figures come from one run", "body": "Our bioreactor page attributes the 3.8% to Test 2 only: \"Plotted directly against the BioDrop with the line of identity, the raw RELEAF points fall along y = x, agreeing to a median paired difference of 3.8%.\" It gives Test 1 as \"y = 1.10x - 0.04, R squared 0.9948\" against the BioDrop, which is a 10% slope error, and it is Test 2 that carries \"each regressing against mass at R squared > 0.999\". Attaching both figures to \"two runs\" overstates what each run showed.", "after": "The two runs are written up as photometer cycle 6 on the bioreactor page. In the second, the raw, uncalibrated output tracked the BioDrop to a median paired difference of 3.8%, with both instruments regressing against TiO2 mass at R squared > 0.999. The first, over a wider range, fit at a slope of 1.10.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-054", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Silica was added to 500 mL of 1% SDS in 2.5 mg steps from 2.5 to 100 mg, forty points in all", "title": "Check this volume against the protocol", "body": "Our Results page describes the written protocol for this run as \"the sample-then-spike titration that holds volume constant at 50 mL\". The arithmetic agrees with 50 mL, not 500. The same page cites the van de Hulst estimate for these particles, \"1 mg/mL of SiO2 at about OD600 2.3\". At 50 mg in 50 mL that is 1 mg/mL and OD 2.3, which is exactly where you report the BioDrop's direct channel stopping. In 500 mL the same point would be 0.23, and nothing in your tables would make sense. One of the two numbers is wrong and it changes every concentration on this page.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-055", "type": "praise", "anchor": "which is a ceiling, not a measurement", "title": "You audited the reference instrument", "body": "This is the best move on the page. You find the reference's own limit, name it in four words, carry the consequence forward in \"Every BioDrop value plotted above OD 2.5 is therefore reconstructed\", and then give the argument that settles it: a weighed inert standard cannot become less turbid when more of it is added. No precedent in the bank questions its reference instrument at all.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen judges two of its own coefficients against each other before collapsing them. Same habit of reading the numbers instead of reporting them.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-056", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "paints TPU onto its seats", "title": "Nobody will know how to do this", "body": "Painting TPU onto a seat is a slicer technique, not an instruction, and it is the step that decides whether the optics sit tight, which 3.3 says gave the biggest improvement in the whole mechanical design. Say what it means: which surfaces, how many layers, and whether the printer needs two materials loaded.", "after": "Filaments PLA Matte for the structure, TPU 95A for the seats and PETG-CF for plate 1, on three plates. The guide prints the lens holder and sensor frame in PETG, with CF optional, and lays TPU over the [named] seat faces for [number] layers, so the printer needs both materials loaded.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-057", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "that scale factor is a six-fold compression", "title": "The factor you measured is 5.57", "body": "Your own sentences give it twice: the factor is 5.573, and the spans are 0.03 to 0.60 against 0.05 to 3.17, which is about 5.5 times. Calling it six-fold rounds your own measurement away from itself in the direction that makes the slim build look worse.", "after": "that scale factor is a five and a half times compression", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-058", "type": "praise", "anchor": "so these constants describe the instruments, not a conversion that can be dropped onto a culture unchecked", "title": "The constants fenced where someone would reuse them", "body": "You give the two numbers a reader would copy, then the physical reason they do not transfer to a culture, then the experiment that would earn them. This is the hardest thing to write on a page that has just reported a good result, and it is the one move TU Darmstadt's precedent page is best at.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-05"], "excerpt_note": "Darmstadt downgrades its reading to qualitative and then defends the downgraded claim as sufficient for the job it has.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-059", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "We believe our current photometer still has room for improvement.", "title": "Part six proposes what part five measured", "body": "Part 6 reads as a plan: we believe we can replace the lens, and we believe that saves two thirds of the length and $70. By then the reader has been through 5.2, 5.3 and 5.4, where a slim build was built, run on forty silica points and recommended for teaching labs. Open this part by saying the slim build exists and was measured, then give the one change that is still a proposal.", "after": "We built a slim photometer to cut the cost of the rest of the system, and 5.3 reports what it measures. Its remaining cost is in the flow cuvette, which is what lets the instrument read the loop live, so that stays. What is still a proposal is replacing the paralleling lens.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "ph-060", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "which leaves the paralleling lens to change", "title": "Your calibration section removed the splitter", "body": "This says the beam splitter is a must-have and only the lens can go. Section 5.3 says of the unit measured on 10 October: \"it carries no reference sensor and has nothing to normalise against LED drift\", and 5.4 then recommends that build for a teaching lab. The same conflict runs through the saving: 6.2 credits $70 to dropping the lens, while 5.3 and 5.4 credit $70 to a build that drops the lens and the splitter. One $70 cannot pay for both, and neither figure has a priced part behind it.", "after": "The beam splitter is what keeps a long run free of drift, so the complex build keeps it. The slim build in 5.3 drops it, and 5.4 says which job each is for. That leaves the paralleling lens as the part the complex build can still lose.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-061", "type": "praise", "anchor": "600 nm LED at 5 V, switched from pin D3 (2.1, 4.1)", "title": "Every row cites the section behind it", "body": "Each row carries the section where the number was established, so a judge checking any one of them lands in the right place. Eleven of the thirteen rows do this. It costs nothing and no winner page in either bank does it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-062", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "These benefits come at a relatively low cost of around $150", "title": "Two prices, no source for either", "body": "The $150 has no costed table behind it, and the $15,000 appears here, on our Measurement page and on our Results page without a model, a date or a quote. A judge who looks up a BioDrop and finds a different figure loses trust in the comparison, and the comparison is the page's main claim. Aachen's photometer page carries its exchange rate and the date it was taken, for a $60 instrument.", "after": "These benefits come at a cost of about $150 [total from Table 1], small beside the prices of commercial laboratory spectrophotometers: the BioDrop [model] we compared against is listed at $15,000 [source, date].", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-063", "type": "cut", "anchor": "It removes the reliance on sampling entirely", "title": "This claim has been made three times", "body": "The scroll panel, the Summary and 1.3 have each said that nothing is withdrawn from the culture. A closing section can assume the reader got there through the page.", "after": "Our photometer can help any student or professor measure the optical density of their solution at 600 nm, live, during growth runs.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-064", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Our photometer can help any student or professor measure the optical density of their solution", "title": "No student or professor has used it yet", "body": "This sentence offers the instrument to a stranger, and nobody outside the hardware team is recorded anywhere on the page as having built, assembled or read it. That is the second ballot question, and it is unanswered on all four of our hardware pages. Aachen 2014 had another iGEM team, Freiburg, repeat part of its calibration, and that is the only answer to this question in the whole evidence bank. The cheapest version is one person outside the sub-team building from 9.1 and writing down where they got stuck.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-05", "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-03"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners name the outside people, state what they wanted it for, and then list their remarks as problems with the device.", "ballot": "Did the team conduct user testing and learn from user feedback?"}, {"id": "ph-065", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Every part is listed in 1.1 and every printed part downloads from 9.1", "title": "The sentence a judge will test", "body": "This is the page's rebuild promise, so walk it as a stranger. 1.1 gives no price and no supplier for the five optical parts. There is no assembly order, no glue, no alignment check and no CAD source, only STLs. There is no firmware link. There is no licence on the designs, and the footer's CC BY 4.0 and repository link cover the wiki text. Four of those are an afternoon each. Until they exist, this sentence promises more than the page gives.", "after": "Every part is listed in 1.1 and every printed part downloads from 9.1. The assembly order is in [guide], the firmware in [repository], the CAD sources in [repository], and the designs are released under [licence].", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06", "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-03"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell names the manual and itemises what is in it. Aachen positions its page as the documentation earlier DIY builds lacked, which is the claim you are making here.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-066", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A light source able to emit several wavelengths has not been purchased, integrated or tested", "title": "Bought, built and tested kept apart", "body": "You separate the sensors you own from the source you do not, with three verbs in rising order, and then say what that leaves untested. A reader cannot mistake a plan for a result here. The 2025 overgraduate winner's own OD sensor paragraph does exactly this.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06"], "excerpt_note": "Built, but neither tested nor integrated in the timeframe, stated in the same paragraph that describes the part."}, {"id": "ph-067", "type": "cut", "anchor": "It was not originally planned to measure across wavelengths", "title": "One limitation said three ways", "body": "The sentence opens by saying the photometer was designed purely for 600 nm, then says it was not planned for several wavelengths, then says it would fall short where several are needed at once. Keep the last, which is the one a reader can act on.", "after": "Our photometer was designed to measure the optical density of the bacteria in our medium, live, at 600 nm. It could likely be converted for another single wavelength, but it would fall short where several wavelengths must be measured at once.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ph-068", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "by turning the LED in its clip during assembly, you can still find an orientation", "title": "This is an assembly step, not a limitation", "body": "The one piece of assembly advice on the page is filed under Limitations, where nobody building the instrument will look for it. It also has no success criterion: a reader turning the LED has no way to know when the path lines up. Your own dashboard gives one, the blank ratio of about 10:1. Move this into 9.1 as a step and give it the target.", "after": "Turn the LED in its clip until the blank ratio of sample to reference reads about [number] on the dashboard, then tighten the mount. The LED does not always sit straight in its socket, so this takes a few minutes.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-069", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Bambu Lab X1 Carbon, 0.4 mm nozzle, textured PEI plate", "title": "Print settings nobody else publishes", "body": "Printer, nozzle, plate, profile, layer height, infill, supports and line width, all given. Somebody with a different printer can match these. TU Darmstadt ships a zip and Aachen ships STLs, and neither publishes a single print setting, so this table is ahead of both precedents.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "ph-070", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "PLA Matte, TPU 95A and PETG-CF, on three plates", "title": "Three filaments here, two in the parts list", "body": "The bill of materials in 1.1 lists only \"Black PLA and black TPU (soft)\", the specification sheet says \"PLA with TPU-lined seats\", and 3.3 explains the design as PLA for the structure with TPU lining. This row adds PETG-CF for the lens holder and sensor frame, which are the two parts that hold the optics. Somebody ordering filament from 1.1 cannot print plate 1.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}], "excerpts": {"hardware-2023-OG-evry-01": {"id": "hardware-2023-OG-evry-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Evry-Paris-Saclay", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/evry-paris-saclay/hardware", "section": "Hardware", "function": "reuse", "text": "Our primary objective was not only to support our project but also to ensure our hardware designs were well-documented. This would allow easy replication and use by the broader community. Our commitment to this philosophy influenced our choices throughout, including the selection of components and the extensive use of 3D printed parts.", "why_it_works": "The need for the hardware and the promise to document it arrive in the same breath, before any device is described. Replication is named as a design constraint that drove component choice, not as an afterthought.", "teaches": ["reuse", "structure", "audience"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-04": {"id": "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/aachen/hardware", "section": "Cost Estimation of MAIK", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Depending on the 3D printing material used, MAIK can be even cheaper in terms of material costs, as can be seen in Table 1. It can also be stated that MAIK is generally an inexpensive measuring unit in which simple color measurements can be carried out with sufficient accuracy.", "why_it_works": "The cost conclusion is tied to the table above it and to a choice the reader controls, the print material. A cost claim that names the variable behind it is one another team can reproduce.", "teaches": ["evidence", "reuse", "reproducibility"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-05": {"id": "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/aachen/hardware", "section": "Results of Cultivating Cells on the Chips", "function": "method", "text": "In order to possibly improve the adhesion of the cells, a slide coated with PDMS was used as the underside of the chip in one of the interaction cycles instead of a plain glass slide. As there was no clear improvement in the adhesion of the cell to PDMS under the microscope compared to glass (see Figure 4), we stayed with the simple glass slide for cost reasons.", "why_it_works": "An attempted improvement that did not work is reported in two sentences, with the reason for reverting given as cost rather than as failure. Naming the alternative and why it lost is more convincing than only showing the winner.", "teaches": ["honesty", "decisions", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-01": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Testing Mk. 1", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To ensure proper bubbling in our bioreactor, we adjusted the current and voltage to the DC motor using a power supply until it produced the desired bubbling rate (2 / sec). However, we realized that these metrics changed depending on the depth of the tubing. We decided to set a standard metric at 100 mL below surface level and found that 9 V and 0.9 A produced the desired bubbling rate. With this test, we were able to establish that a 9V battery would be a sufficient power source for mark 1.", "why_it_works": "A target is set (two bubbles per second), the measurement is found to depend on tubing depth, so a standard depth is fixed and only then is the working voltage and current reported. Defining the condition is what makes the number repeatable.", "teaches": ["reproducibility", "evidence", "measurement"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-02": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-02", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Lessons Learned from Mk.1 (Internally)", "function": "limitation", "text": "Considering the time we spent using a Dremel tool to sand parts down, it would probably be worth our while in the future to add tolerances and extra space to our parts due to 3D printer error that can cause abrasiveness between moving parts or prints that have fits to them.", "why_it_works": "A build annoyance is turned into a transferable design rule: add tolerance for printer error on parts that have to fit or move. The lesson is stated as what they would do next time, not as an excuse.", "teaches": ["honesty", "reuse", "decisions"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-03": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Learning through iHP Integration & User Feedback", "function": "audience-framing", "text": "We attempted to gain user feedback on Mk.1 from local collaborators that were external to our team. To begin, we took our bioreactor to the Biomedical Research Facility at UBC with our iHP contact Maggie Chopra, we identified four key areas of progression that future work can target:", "why_it_works": "User testing is written as an event with a place, a named contact outside the team, and a count of what came back. The four areas are then listed as problems with the device, not as compliments.", "teaches": ["user testing", "honesty", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-01": {"id": "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TUDarmstadt", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/tudarmstadt/hardware", "section": "Motivation", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Microgravity significantly alters cellular growth, mobility, gene expression and biofilm formation [1, 2]. As parabolic flights are too short for long biological experiments and launches are expensive and limited, random positioning machines (RPMs) are used to simulate microgravity for an extended time in the lab. However, as commercial RPMs cost around 8.000$-50.000$ [3] they are inaccessible to many student labs and iGEM teams. Luckily, there are existing DIY solutions [3]; We discovered two GitLab projects describing DIY RPMs with a protocol and STL-data for printing (by Wahab Kawafi and Core Electronics). However, neither was validated to prove real microgravity simulation, and most published RPMs are either too expensive or described in overly complex, inaccessible papers [4].", "why_it_works": "The need is built in four moves: why microgravity matters, why existing methods fail, what commercial kit costs, and what the two existing DIY designs still lack. The gap the project fills is the last clause, that neither was validated.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-04": {"id": "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TUDarmstadt", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/tudarmstadt/hardware", "section": "Discussion", "function": "validation", "text": "To test whether this difference was noticeable, several team members, without prior knowledge of which tube had been rotated, were asked to identify the RPM sample. Everyone could correctly recognize it.", "why_it_works": "A qualitative effect is made into a test by removing the testers' knowledge of which sample was which. Two sentences convert a picture into evidence, and the design of the check is what does it.", "teaches": ["validation", "evidence", "honesty"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-05": {"id": "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TUDarmstadt", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/tudarmstadt/hardware", "section": "User insight and usage", "function": "audience-framing", "text": "We demonstrated the Random Positioning Machine (RPM) to team members Eva Schätzlein and David Sipos, who expressed great interest in using it for ground-based experiments simulating microgravity. They provided several valuable technical remarks and suggestions for further optimization:", "why_it_works": "Outside researchers are named, their project and their interest are stated, and the feedback that follows is a list of four concrete technical limits of the device. This is what user testing looks like when the users are the people who would run it.", "teaches": ["user testing", "audience", "honesty"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06": {"id": "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TUDarmstadt", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/tudarmstadt/hardware", "section": "OD Measurement", "function": "limitation", "text": "To accurately monitor the cell density of Bacillus subtilis culture, we developed a DIY optical density (OD) sensor. It uses an orange LED (emission spectrum 590–625 nm) and a photodiode to measure the turbidity of the liquid culture. We successfully built a working circuit for the optical sensor, but could not test or integrate it into the bioreactor within the project timeframe.", "why_it_works": "A component is described with its part-level detail and then reported unfinished in the same paragraph: built, but neither tested nor integrated in time. The admission sits with the thing it concerns rather than in a future-work list.", "teaches": ["honesty", "structure", "limitations"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04": {"id": "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Cornell", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/cornell/hardware", "section": "Build for PRoSPER - and for your own ideas", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Each COB LED module is equipped with a 12V 16W COB LED panel capable of delivering up to 1000 lumens of brightness. At the working distance within ARC, a single panel can provide up to 37 klux of brightness, which is more than sufficient to support Synechococcus which requires 3000 lux. In the final tests, only 20% brightness was needed to achieve optimal illumination with 2 modules.", "why_it_works": "Three numbers in order: what the part can deliver, what the organism needs, and what was actually used. Stating the requirement next to the capability turns a specification into evidence of fitness.", "teaches": ["evidence", "measurement", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06": {"id": "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Cornell", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/cornell/hardware", "section": "Build for PRoSPER - and for your own ideas", "function": "reuse", "text": "Meanwhile, we also prepared a general operating manual containing detailed assembly guidelines and operation guidelines. It contains detailed descriptions of the precautions in operation, the assembly of autoclave configuration, and the specific structural differences among different variants.", "why_it_works": "The deliverable another team needs is named and its contents are itemised: assembly, operation, precautions, and the differences between variants. One sentence tells the reader the manual exists and what is in it.", "teaches": ["reuse", "reproducibility", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Humboldt Berlin", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:Humboldt_Berlin/Hardware", "section": "Introduction", "function": "page-opening", "text": "The openPBR is a low-cost, easy to assemble and reliable option to cultivate phototrophic organisms. We have developed it as an open-source platform to operate as you need it. It comes in several setups, from nine cultivation vessels to three flat panels with a minimum width of 15 mm and up to a volume of 190 ml. It provides users with the ability to measure optical density at wavelengths from 400 nm to 800 nm, online and provides a .csv file output. All parts are openly available and even gas supply is exactly adjustable through a rotameter, depending on the needs of your organism and setup. We want to offer scientists a simple and versatile cultivation platform.", "why_it_works": "The opening doubles as a specification sheet: configurations, vessel widths, volumes, wavelength range, output format. A reader can decide from one paragraph whether the instrument fits their organism.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure", "reproducibility"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-04": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Humboldt Berlin", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:Humboldt_Berlin/Hardware", "section": "Pricing", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Since we are a non-profit team of our own, we know that financing is one of the limiting and most problematic challenges of this contest. So it was important to us, that every iGEM team who wants to continue our project ,or any new project using a phototrophic organism, has a low-price but efficient way to fulfill their great ideas. For only 588 € it is possible to rebuild our bioreactor that offers many opportunities to design a set-up tailored to the needs of innovative projects.", "why_it_works": "Gives the reason for publishing a price before giving the price: other iGEM teams are short of money. The total is a single figure a team can hold against its own budget.", "teaches": ["reuse", "audience-framing", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-01": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2014, "track": null, "url": "https://2014.igem.org/Team:Aachen/OD/F_device", "section": "OD/F Device", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Measuring Optical Density (OD) or absorbance is one of the indispensable elements in the field of microbiology. One question that has to be answered often is how many cells are in a suspension? Here, the OD can give a hint. However, the commercially available [http://www.laboratory-equipment.com/laboratory-equipment/cell-density-meter.php OD meters] are expensive and limit its application and usage in low budget institutions.\nTherefore, here we present our OD/F Device. The device is specifically designed for biohackspaces, Do It Yourself (DIY), community laboratories and schools. With our OD/F Device, we aim to enable precise and inexpensive scientific research.", "why_it_works": "Three sentences set up the measurement, the question it answers and the barrier, which is price, before the device is named. The second paragraph then names the readers it exists for. The instrument is justified by who cannot afford the alternative.", "teaches": ["structure", "audience-framing", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-03": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2014, "track": null, "url": "https://2014.igem.org/Team:Aachen/OD/F_device", "section": "Achievements", "function": "reuse", "text": "Have we been re-inventing the wheel? No! In fact, you can find some DIY posts for turbidity meters such as [http://www.thingiverse.com/thing:74415 turbidity sensors]. However, a proper assessment of their linearity as well as a calculated OD-value are missing.", "why_it_works": "Asks the question a judge is already thinking, then answers it by naming the prior art and the one thing it failed to document. Positions the page as the missing documentation rather than the missing invention.", "teaches": ["evidence", "reuse", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-04": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2014, "track": null, "url": "https://2014.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Notebook/Engineering/ODF", "section": "Technical Components", "function": "section-intro", "text": "While the casing and the cuvette holder are custom made, most of the parts are pre-made and can be readily bought. The previous section lists all needed parts. To get all these parts for creating your own OD/F Device is easy using the internet. Thus all potential customers have a market access to the used components.", "why_it_works": "Introduces the parts table by splitting the build into custom and off-the-shelf and saying the off-the-shelf half can simply be bought. A reader knows what the table is for before reading a row of it.", "teaches": ["structure", "reuse", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2014, "track": null, "url": "https://2014.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Notebook/Engineering/ODF", "section": "Experiments", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "It is interesting to observer that function $g$, mapping the true OD of our device to the true OD of the photospectrometer, are close together for both P. putida and S. cerivisae, as seen by the regression coefficient. In fact, 3.416 and 3.461 are such close together, that the minor deviation could be just measuring inaccuracy. Therefore, we fix the regression coefficient for converting true OD of our device to true OD of the photospectrometer to an average of 3.432 .", "why_it_works": "Two independently measured calibration coefficients are reported, then judged: the gap between them is small enough to be measurement error, so they are averaged into one fixed constant. The reasoning for collapsing them is shown rather than assumed.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "honesty"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2014, "track": null, "url": "https://2014.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Notebook/Engineering/ODF", "section": "Construction Steps", "function": "method", "text": "Next we want to assemble the cuvette holders. On the side with the square hole attach the light-to-frequency sensor with glue. For the OD case place the orange LED opposite, or for fluorescence, the LED in the hole in the bottom. Make sure to close any remaining open hole! Please attach a piece of filter foil (approx. $7 \\times 7 mm^2$) from the inside in front of the light to frequency converter. Forceps is highly recommended.", "why_it_works": "An assembly step written for someone holding the parts: what to glue where, what changes between the OD and the fluorescence build, a dimension for the filter foil, a warning not to leave a hole open, and the tool that makes it possible.", "teaches": ["reproducibility", "method", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-01": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TU Darmstadt", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:TU_Darmstadt/Tech", "section": "Do it yourself", "function": "page-opening", "text": "In order to automatically induce at the right moment, continuous measurement of the optical density (OD600) of the cell culture is required. We have developed and produced a low-cost OD600-sensor we called SensorBrick. This SensorBrick is connected in between the bioreactor and the syringe pump. The SensorBrick is compatible with any setup of a bioreactor and a syringe pump system and can be used by any other iGEM teams, that are interested in automating processes.", "why_it_works": "Opens on the job the sensor has to do, names it, says where in the rig it sits, and ends by saying who else can use it. The reuse claim is made in the opening rather than left to a closing section.", "teaches": ["clarity", "reuse", "audience-framing"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-03": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TU Darmstadt", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:TU_Darmstadt/Tech", "section": "Do it yourself", "function": "validation", "text": "Now that the SensorBrick is calibrated, the value of the SensorBrick can be inserted into the given function, so that an absolute value of the OD can be obtained. To verify our calibration we ran another two experiments and compared the data to the photometer. We grew E. coli (Fig. 5) and then transformed E. coli with the dual expression vector (Fig. 6).", "why_it_works": "Separates calibration from validation in one sentence: having fitted the transform, they ran two further experiments and compared again. Naming the held-out runs is what makes the earlier coefficient mean something.", "teaches": ["validation", "method", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-05": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TU Darmstadt", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:TU_Darmstadt/Tech", "section": "Do it yourself", "function": "limitation", "text": "Fig. 6 shows that a calibrated SenorBrick determines approximate values above an OD600 of 0.3. We learned that it is necessary to calibrate the SensorBrick with the used bacteria strain individually. It can not be universally calibrated, especially when fluorescent proteins are present. In order to achieve this, the sensor would need to persists of a monochromator. Therefore, the OD600 measurement by the SensorBrick can only be seen as qualitative values. This still suits the purpose of determining the exact moment for the web app to initiate the induction.", "why_it_works": "Gives the threshold above which the reading is usable, draws the general rule (it cannot be calibrated once for every strain), names the part that would be needed to fix it, downgrades its own claim to qualitative, then says why the downgraded claim still does the job.", "teaches": ["limitation", "honesty", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}}};
